{"candidates":[{"claim":"Ownership-based allocation is net-beneficial: it systematically prunes the combinatorial explosion of rival-programme crossovers, and the resulting efficiency is a precondition—rather than a barrier—for discovering cross-cutting explanations, because cross-cutting explanations require high confidence in the source-programme facts being merged, which ownership best provides.","counterconditions":["If the same rival programme, once given ownership of a problem lineage, is empirically observed to block or delete references from other programmes (rather than merely failing to add them), the pruning mechanism has become a suppression mechanism.","If cross-cutting explanations that are generated by defectors (e.g. an ad-hoc aliasing of SRC_001 and SRC_003) are found to outperform owner-produced explanations on external tests, the pruning-via-efficiency defence fails.","If a non-owner programme had a verified source-memory claim that was structurally necessary to the parent artifact but was excluded by ownership routing, the precondition defence is refuted."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"A single reasoning system cannot run every rival hypothesis on every datum. Ownership assigns a problem to the lineage that produced the parent artifact, so that artefact's internal invariants (e.g. school-2 unifier consolidation of ba687c... mechanist outputs) carry forward with minimal friction. By localising search, the system quickly builds a high-confidence substrate within each programme. A cross-cutting explanation, when it does emerge, can then piggyback on the reliable substrate of each owner rather than being discovered by a globally random walk that would starve every programme. Suppression is therefore not systematic; it is an incidental cost of a precondition.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":